<SolutionPage
  title="Sleep Optimization"
  parent="Mind"
  oneliner="Poor sleep is the most reliable accelerant of cognitive decline you can choose. Everything else is downstream."
  slug="sleep"
>
  <h2>The Problem</h2>
  <p>
    Sleep is when the brain clears metabolic waste through the glymphatic system, consolidates
    memory, and resets the hormonal rhythms that govern your next day. Every hour of chronic
    sleep debt is a withdrawal against future cognitive capital — a debt that compounds and
    doesn't fully repay.
  </p>
  <p>
    Most men optimize their training, their diet, their supplements — and then cripple all of it
    with 5–6 hours of fragmented sleep. Cortisol is elevated. Testosterone synthesis is suppressed.
    Memory consolidation is incomplete. Neuroinflammation accumulates. And they wonder why they
    still feel off.
  </p>
  <p>
    Sleep is not a passive state. It is the most active recovery protocol available to you — and
    it costs nothing except discipline about your evening environment. The Mind pillar of 4M starts
    here.
  </p>

  <h2>The Insulting Behaviors</h2>
  <p>If you're doing any of these, your sleep is worse than you think — even if you feel fine.</p>
  <ul class="insult-list">
    <li>Bright artificial overhead lighting in the hours after sundown — suppresses melatonin production</li>
    <li>Screens in bed or within 60 minutes of sleep — blue light tells your brain it's noon</li>
    <li>Caffeine after 2pm — its half-life is 5–6 hours; it's still in your system at midnight</li>
    <li>Alcohol within 3 hours of bed — it kills deep sleep architecture even if it helps you fall asleep</li>
    <li>Inconsistent bedtime and wake time — your circadian clock runs on anchors, not approximations</li>
    <li>Bedroom warmer than 68°F or with ambient light — core temperature must drop to initiate deep sleep</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class="section-eliminate">Eliminate the Insulting Behavior</h2>
  <p>Fix the environment first. Supplements are the last layer, not the first.</p>
  <div class="card-grid">
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Step 1 — Lights Out by 10:30pm</h3>
      <p>Consistent bedtime and wake time are the fastest way to reset circadian rhythm. Weekends too. The clock doesn't take days off.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Step 2 — No Screens 30 Minutes Before Bed</h3>
      <p>Switch to low-amber lighting, journaling, reading on paper, or breath work. The nervous system needs a wind-down ramp.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Step 3 — Cool, Dark Bedroom</h3>
      <p>Blackout curtains. Under 68°F. No chargers or screens at bedside. This is your most important recovery environment.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Step 4 — Eliminate Alcohol on Weeknights</h3>
      <p>Even two drinks suppress REM sleep. Trade the ritual — herbal tea, sparkling water, magnesium glycinate before bed.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Step 5 — SleepRestore</h3>
      <p>The My4MLife sleep-stack formula — formulated to support sleep onset and deep-sleep architecture as the final layer on top of a clean environment.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</SolutionPage>
